branches.task:
IRIS-DOMAIN / ROUTING REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Review this M3 loop-driver + report + routing design for the IRIS analyst-agent optimization loop:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Judge ONLY through the lens of whether ROUTING lands changes on the REAL IRIS artifacts and whether the report is actionable for an IRIS operator. (1) Do the route targets map to actual IRIS surfaces — is a Type-1 prompt edit a real PR against a real IRIS prompt file, is a Type-2 habit/reflection a real store the operator controls, are Type-4 retrieval-corpus and Type-5 code correctly NOT auto-PR'd? (2) The loop lives in dotdev but the system-under-test is IRIS (a separate repo, classdojo/iris) — does cross-repo routing (PR into iris, issue into iris vs dotdev) actually work and land in the right place? (3) Does the report surface the IRIS-specific risk context (cross-lane note, SOLE-DOMAIN, metric-disambiguation blind spot) where the operator will actually see it before approving? (4) Is 'landed = merged+approved' realistic given the operator also owns the IRIS repo? Output AGREE or OBJECT per element with a concrete fix.

SKEPTIC / RISK REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Stress-test this M3 loop-driver + report + routing + state design:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Adversarial lens only: (1) APPROVAL BYPASS / RUBBER-STAMP — can a candidate get routed without a genuine human decision (default-approve, batch-approve, no-action-means-yes)? Is the fail-safe correct (no action MUST mean not-routed)? (2) STATE CORRUPTION — concurrent runs, a hand-edited state file, a crash between route and ledger-write; what breaks and is it recoverable? (3) WRONG-TARGET / BLAST — can an approved diff route to the wrong prompt/lane/repo, or a human-EDITed diff silently re-enter automated net-attribution and corrupt the next baseline? (4) SECRETS — warehouse creds + LLM keys must run the eval; can they leak into state, reports, PR bodies, or CI logs? (5) LOOP-HEALTH HALT — if the sentinel fails, does the driver actually stop emitting, or can candidates still slip through? (6) AUTO-MODE — is deferring it genuinely safe, or does any auto-path sneak in? Output AGREE or OBJECT per element with the concrete failure mode + fix.

phases.task:
Three specialists reviewed the proposed M3 loop-driver + morning report + routing + state design (round {args.round}).

PROPOSAL UNDER REVIEW:
{args.proposal}

SPECIALIST REVIEWS:
{steps.panel.output}

Decide consensus. Rules: consensus=true ONLY if NO specialist raised a material/blocking DESIGN objection (minor wording nits and human-input OPEN items do not block). Fold every ACCEPTED fix into revised_selections; reject fixes that conflict with the locked eval (DL-0022), the candidate-generator (DL-0023), DL-0020's top-5 cap, or DL-0021's portable-loop framing, and say why in critique. If you materially revise the text, consensus MUST be false (do not rubber-stamp unreviewed revisions). Keep it v1-scoped. Output ONLY JSON:
{
 "consensus": true|false,
 "revised_selections": "<the full, current best M3 design incorporating accepted fixes>",
 "changes_from_proposal": "<what changed vs the proposal, or 'none'>",
 "critique": "<remaining open objections that block consensus, or 'none'>"
}
